Apiary has been installed.

WHAT THIS PACKAGE INSTALLED

  /usr/local/libexec/apiary/    the four daemons and the two operator
                                tools, plus the optional MCP connector
  /usr/local/etc/rc.d/          the five rc.d scripts (none enabled)
  /usr/local/etc/apiary/        four sample configuration files and the
                                sample README, installed as .sample
                                files so pkg upgrade will not
                                overwrite an edited copy
  /var/db/apiary/               runtime state: the Raft data directory
                                and the ISO image store
  /var/run/apiary/              the Raft socket directory
  /var/log/apiary/              the daemon logfiles

WHAT THIS PACKAGE DELIBERATELY DID NOT DO

  It did not write any configuration. The real config files
  (raftd.json, managerd.json, frontend.json, restshimd.json) are
  written by `apiaryctl install`, which preserves every field that is
  already set rather than overwriting it. Shipping a config with the
  package would put a second source of truth next to the one the tool
  owns.

  It did not issue a TLS certificate. managerd's serving certificate
  is issued in Go by `apiaryctl install`, on every Comb including a
  single-node one, because TLS being on by default is a decision and
  not a consequence of there being peers.

  It did not write a PAM policy. /etc/pam.d/apiary is system
  configuration outside the package's own prefix, and the project's
  `make setup-pam` writes it only if it is absent, so a hand-edited
  policy is never clobbered. Without it, managerd will refuse to
  start with -pam-service set.

  It did not enable any service. Run `sysrc apiary_raftd_enable=YES`
  and the rest yourself, in the order the rc.d scripts declare:
  raftd, then managerd, then frontend and restshimd.

NEXT STEPS

  1. Write the configuration:

	/usr/local/libexec/apiary/apiaryctl install

     Run it with no arguments first to see what it would do. It needs
     a ZFS pool, a VLAN uplink and a bhyve bridge; it will tell you
     if any of them are missing.

  2. Create the PAM policy, if you have not already:

	make setup-pam

  3. Enable and start the services:

	sysrc apiary_raftd_enable=YES
	sysrc apiary_managerd_enable=YES
	sysrc apiary_frontend_enable=YES
	sysrc apiary_restshimd_enable=YES
	service apiary_raftd start
	service apiary_managerd start
	service apiary_frontend start
	service apiary_restshimd start

  4. Log in to the web UI with any existing UNIX account. On a Comb
     whose role map is empty, whoever logs in first becomes Admin.

  The full bring-up walkthrough, including the parts this package does
  not cover, is in the project's docs/bootstrap.md.
